\section{The spherical benchmark}\label{sec:S2}

We reimplemented the analytical model of \citet{jas2026} with their definitions. A tangential current dipole of << F.g1a_q_nam >>~nAm sits in a spherical conductor (head radius << F.g1a_h_mm >>~mm, brain radius << F.g1a_b_mm >>~mm), and point magnetometers measure the radial field on the scalp (OPM) and << F.g1a_xi_squid_mm >>~mm above it (SQUID). The single-channel SNR is the peak field over the noise standard deviation $\sigma$, and $\sigma_{\mathrm{OPM}} = \eta\,\sigma_{\mathrm{SQUID}}$. The equal-SNR depth is the exact root of the preprint's Eq.~3. We checked the peak field against the full Sarvas field maximized over the sensor sphere (largest relative difference << F.g1a_eq1_vs_sarvas_rel_err_opm >>) and against the sphere model of MNE-Python \citep[<< F.g1a_eq1_vs_mne_rel_err_opm >>;][]{gramfort2013}.

The reimplementation reproduced the preprint. At $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> the exact equal-SNR depth is << F.g1a_deq_eta3_mm_3dp >>~mm. The preprint prints << F.g1a_fig3_deq_printed_mm >>~mm in its text and caption, and its figure draws << F.g1a_fig3_deq_drawn_mm >>~mm; the difference reflects the preprint's rounding and grid, not a difference of model. A crossing exists only for $\eta$ between << F.g1a_eta0_exact >> and << F.g1a_eta1_exact >> (printed as << F.g1a_eta0_printed >> and << F.g1a_eta1_printed >>). Only $\eta$ enters the equal-SNR depth: scaling both noises together leaves it at << F.g1a_deq_sigma_independent_mm >>~mm.

On the real head, the peak field at the best OPM over that at the best Neuromag magnetometer falls with depth as the sphere predicts, once the sphere is given the real median standoffs: << F.g2_matched_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm for the OPMs and << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm for the magnetometers. For the dense array this ratio fell from << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_10_15_ratio >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm bin to << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_55_60_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm, against << F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_10_15_ratio >> and << F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_55_60_ratio >> for the sphere.

The bridge between the two models uses the sphere's own metric: peak-channel SNR against the magnetometers, with sensor noise only. With it, the realistic equal-SNR depth at $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> was << F.g2_bridge_deq_matched_eta3_mm >>~mm for the site-matched array and << F.g2_bridge_deq_dense_eta3_mm >>~mm for the dense array, against << F.g2_bridge_deq_sphere_real_eta3_mm >>~mm for the sphere with the real standoffs. For $\eta$ from << F.g2_bridge_common_eta_min >> to << F.g2_bridge_common_eta_max >>, the real arrays' depths lay within << F.g2_bridge_deq_arrays_minus_sphere_real_mm_range >>~mm of the sphere's (Fig.~\ref{fig:S1}B). Outside that window they differed.

The OPMs were ahead at every depth for $\eta \le$ << F.g2_bridge_dense_eta_opm_ahead_max >>, where the sphere still predicts a deep crossing (<< F.g2_bridge_deq_sphere_real_eta2p25_mm >>~mm at $\eta$~=~<< F.g2_bridge_dense_eta_opm_ahead_max >>). The reason is that the real peak-field ratio levels off at depth (<< F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_60_65_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_60_65_mm >>~mm), while the sphere's keeps falling (<< F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_60_65_ratio >>). Neuromag's magnetometers were ahead at every depth for $\eta \ge$ << F.g2_bridge_matched_eta_squid_ahead_min >> (site-matched) or << F.g2_bridge_dense_eta_squid_ahead_min >> (dense). The sphere is therefore right about the geometry of magnetometers near its reference $\eta$.

Against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> planar gradiometers, the ratio with sensor noise only barely changed with depth, so the sphere describes only the magnetometer part of the comparison. What it leaves open is $\eta$ itself, which the explicit noise model of Section~\ref{sec:S3} replaces.

For head size, we placed the newborn, << F.g3a_infant1yr_age_years >>-year, << F.g3a_child8yr_age_years >>-year and adult spheres of the preprint in a SQUID shell that follows each head at << F.lit_xi_squid_mm >>~mm, as in the preprint. As an additional contrast, we placed all of them in the adult shell of radius << F.g3a_fixed_shell_radius >>~mm. With the size-following shell, the equal-SNR depth at $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> hardly moved from adult to newborn (<< F.g3a_follow_eta3_deq_range >>~mm). The normalized depth, however, rose from << F.g3a_adult_follow_eta3_norm_1dp >> to << F.g3a_newborn_follow_eta3_norm_1dp >>, because the brain and the extracerebral layer (<< F.lit_table1_extracerebral_range_mm >>~mm in the preprint's heads) shrink.

With one adult shell for every head, size and fit are confounded. The OPM was then ahead at every depth of the newborn and << F.g3a_infant1yr_age_years >>-year brains up to $\eta$~=~<< F.g3a_newborn_fixed_eta0_1dp >> and << F.g3a_infant1yr_fixed_eta0_1dp >>, respectively. The sphere's prediction for children is thus conditional on a shell that follows the head at a constant gap.

